;;
;; REPL/RedTeam/[RT-H]_InputDomain.repl — FAMILY H: input domain.
;;
;; "What happens at the edges of what an argument is allowed to be?"
;;
;; Deliberately NOT a fuzzing campaign. A full gate run is ~7 minutes, so random search is an
;; expensive way to find what reading the guards tells you for free. These attacks are targeted at
;; specific gaps identified by reading `SWPU|X>SWAP`, which validates its inputs one at a time and
;; therefore cannot see any property OF THE SET.
;;
;; Run: cd REPL && pact "RedTeam/[RT-H]_InputDomain.repl"
;;
(load "../deploy-stage00.repl")
(load "../deploy-stage01.repl")
(load "../Stage_01/[6.2]_DPTF.repl")
(load "../Stage_01/[6.3]_SWP.repl")

;;|| NEXT >
;;
(begin-tx "RT-H-001 - swap a token for itself, and swap a token twice")
(env-gasmodel "table")(env-gaslimit 100000000)(env-gas 0)
(namespace "ouronet-ns")
;;==== RT-H-001 · 01 · set properties the per-item validator cannot see ====
(print "--- [RT-H-001 · 01 · set properties the per-item validator cannot see] ---")
;;<<RT-H-001>> FAMILY: H | STATUS: REFUSED
;;HYPOTHESIS: `SWPU|X>SWAP` — the capability guarding the NO-SLIPPAGE swap path, which is the one a
;;            caller reaches by passing the `-1.0` sentinel — validates the output is on the pool,
;;            that swapping is enabled, that input-ids and input-amounts are the same LENGTH, and
;;            then loops over the inputs checking each is on the pool with a valid amount. Every one
;;            of those is a per-ITEM property. Nothing checks a property of the SET. So two things
;;            should be constructible: `output-id` also appearing in `input-ids` (swap a token for
;;            itself), and the same id appearing twice (pay one token, be credited for two).
;;METHOD:     drive both through the live Talos client on a real pool, and record WHICH guard
;;            refuses. The identity of the refusing guard is the entire result here.
;;RESULT:     both refused — and NEITHER by a guard that is about the set. Details below. The
;;            defences are real but incidental, which is a materially weaker position than it looks
;;            from a green test.
;;
;;WHAT REFUSES THE SELF-SWAP, and it is not a validator. `output-id` in `input-ids` passes every
;;check in `SWPU|X>SWAP`: it is on the pool, its amount is positive, the lengths match. The swap
;;proceeds INTO the curve math, which computes an output of exactly ZERO — adding and removing the
;;same token on a constant-function curve nets to nothing — and the transfer of that zero is then
;;refused by `DPTF::UEV_Amount`'s `(> amount 0.0)`. So the transaction is rejected by a
;;ZERO-AMOUNT TRANSFER RULE, three modules away from the mistake, and only because the arithmetic
;;happened to land on exactly 0.0.
;;
;;    That is the finding. The system is not protected from self-swaps by a rule forbidding them;
;;    it is protected by the curve returning zero. Any future change that makes a self-swap return
;;    a non-zero positive amount — a different curve, an amplifier, a fee rebate, a rounding
;;    direction — turns this into value creation with no guard in the way. There is no
;;    `output-id NOT IN input-ids` check anywhere on this path.
;;
;;WHAT REFUSES THE DUPLICATE is also not a uniqueness rule: "Only a single Input can be used in
;;Stable Swap" is a POOL-TYPE rule. It happens to make duplicates unconstructible on a STABLE pool
;;because stable pools take one input at all. It says nothing about weighted pools, which accept
;;several inputs by design. SCOPE STATED HONESTLY: this block does not reach a weighted pool, so
;;the duplicate case is pinned only for the stable shape, and the weighted shape is an open
;;follow-up rather than a result claimed here.
(env-sigs [{ "key": "PK_AncientHodler", "caps": [] }])
(let
    (
        (ref-TS01-C3:module{TalosStageOne_ClientThreeV4} TS01-C3)
        (ref-DPTF:module{DemiourgosPactTrueFungibleV2} DPTF)
        (ref-SWP:module{SwapperV4} SWP)
        (pool:string "S|DLK-98c486052a51|OURO-98c486052a51|DWK-98c486052a51")
        (tok:string "OURO-98c486052a51")
        (other:string "DLK-98c486052a51")
        (me:string KST.ANHD)
    )
    (let
        (
            (mine-before:decimal (ref-DPTF::UR_AccountSupply tok me))
            (pool-before:[decimal] (ref-SWP::UR_PoolTokenSupplies pool))
        )
        (map print
            [
                (format "<<RT-H-001>> holding {} {} against pool {}" [mine-before tok pool-before])
                ;;ATTACK 1 -- swap a token for ITSELF. Refused by the zero-amount transfer rule,
                ;;which is what the message names. Asserting the real message rather than a hoped-for
                ;;"self swap not allowed" is the whole point: the latter does not exist.
                (expect-failure "<<RT-H-001>> a self-swap is stopped by the ZERO-AMOUNT rule, not by a set check"
                    "is not a Valid Transaction amount"
                    (ref-TS01-C3::SWP|C_MultiSwapNoSlippage me me pool [tok] [100.0] tok))
                ;;ATTACK 2 -- the same input id twice. Refused by a POOL-TYPE rule, not uniqueness.
                (expect-failure "<<RT-H-001>> a duplicated input is stopped by the POOL-TYPE rule, not by uniqueness"
                    "Only a single Input can be used in Stable Swap"
                    (ref-TS01-C3::SWP|C_MultiSwapNoSlippage me me pool [other other] [50.0 50.0] tok))
                ;;"AND NOTHING MOVED" CANNOT BE ASSERTED HERE, and the reason is worth recording
                ;;because the first draft of this block got it wrong. Two assertions were written to
                ;;show the refusals were total; both FAILED, showing the caller debited by exactly
                ;;the 100.0 self-swap input and the pool's OURO reserve up 0.53:
                ;;
                ;;    caller  389170.206086... -> 389070.206086...
                ;;    pool    [.., 901.591515.., ..] -> [.., 902.121515.., ..]
                ;;
                ;;That is RULE 9 -- `expect-failure` catches the abort MID-TRANSACTION and does NOT
                ;;roll back the writes that preceded it (`memories/2026-09-11-expect-failure-does-
                ;;not-roll-back-writes.md`). On chain the whole transaction reverts, so no value is
                ;;lost; the partial state is a REPL artefact of catching the abort. "The refusal is
                ;;total" is therefore guaranteed by Pact's transaction semantics and is not
                ;;something this suite can demonstrate -- so it is not claimed.
                ;;
                ;;What the partial write DOES show is real and worth keeping: the self-swap is
                ;;refused LATE, after the input has been debited and the curve evaluated. The guard
                ;;is not a cheap up-front rejection; the caller pays for the whole computation of a
                ;;swap that could never have succeeded.
                ;;NON-VACUITY: a well-formed swap on this same pool DOES work, so the refusals above
                ;;are about the malformed input sets and not about the pool being unusable.
                (expect "<<RT-H-001>> ...while a well-formed swap on the same pool succeeds"
                    true (> (length (ref-TS01-C3::SWP|C_SingleSwapNoSlippage me me pool other 10.0 tok)) 0))
            ]
        )
    )
    ""
)
(env-sigs [])
(rollback-tx)
